# Backend API. No trailing slash and no /api suffix: the client appends /api.
# Use a LAN IP for a physical device; localhost resolves to the phone itself.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.117:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth client ids — one per platform, all from the SAME Google project
# the backend verifies against (solvedblue-staging for dev and preview).
#
# Firebase console > Project settings > General > Your apps gives you the
# Firebase values below. The OAuth clients themselves live in
# Google Cloud Console > APIs & Services > Credentials, in the same project.
#
#   Web      - needed in the browser, and as the fallback. Already exists:
#              it is the one that makes the web app work.
#   iOS      - create "iOS" client type, bundle id com.spyder.mobile
#   Android  - create "Android" client type, package com.spyder.mobile,
#              plus the signing SHA-1 from `eas credentials`
#
# LEAVE A LINE BLANK RATHER THAN GUESSING, but know that a blank is treated as
# missing on purpose (see envClientId in src/auth/useGoogleSignIn.ts): the sign
# -in button is then disabled on that platform instead of failing oddly.
#
# THE REDIRECT URI, which is the part everyone gets wrong:
#
#   On a real build, expo-auth-session uses
#       com.spyder.mobile:/oauthredirect
#   derived from the Android/iOS application id, NOT from `scheme`, and NOT
#   https://auth.expo.io/... — the Expo auth proxy is legacy and this SDK does
#   not use it. app.json claims `com.spyder.mobile` as a URL scheme so the
#   redirect can come back into the app.
#
#   iOS and Android OAuth client types have no redirect-URI box in the console:
#   iOS is matched on the bundle id, Android on package name + SHA-1. So there
#   is nothing to paste for them. Only a *Web* client has that box, and the
#   mobile app does not use a web redirect.
#
#   In Expo Go the redirect becomes exp://<lan-ip>:8081/--/ , which Google
#   refuses for an installed app. Google sign-in therefore needs a development
#   build; it cannot work in Expo Go on SDK 58.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=

# Firebase client config, from the same project. Used only to exchange the
# Google token for a Firebase ID token, which is what the backend verifies.
# Must be the project the backend's FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID points at, or the
# token's audience will not match.
#
# Not secrets: EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the bundle and readable by
# anyone who unpacks the app. Firebase client config is designed to be public.
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=<project-id>.firebaseapp.com
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign in with Apple needs nothing here.
#
# The backend verifies Apple's identity token against Apple's published keys
# itself (spyder-backend/src/services/appleAuth.ts), so there is no Firebase
# hop and no client id to configure. All it needs is a paid Apple Developer
# account and a development build: EAS turns on the Sign In with Apple
# capability from `ios.usesAppleSignIn` in app.json.
#
# The backend accepts audience com.spyder.mobile by default; set
# APPLE_BUNDLE_IDS there only if the bundle id ever changes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
